  Scenario: Ascender's namespace denies traffic by default and lets through only the flows its policies declare, and job pods reach the resolver and the world on 22 and 443 alone
    Given the namespace's default deny, the policies of both applications, the proxies' policy and the operator's rendered pod
    When each policy's rules are read and the flows between Ascender's pods, the operator, the database, the proxies, Prometheus, the API server, the kubelet and the world are evaluated
    Then the namespace holds the default deny and is not listed; the operator takes the kubelet on 6789 and reaches the API server alone; the web pods take the proxies, the kubelet and the task pods on 8052; every pod of the instance reaches the resolver and the database's instances on 5432; the task pods reach the web pods on 8052, the API server and the world on 443 and 22; the job pods reach the resolver and the world on 443 and 22, each world port by a policy of its own with its reason, and nothing in the cluster, and take nothing; the proxies reach the web pods on 8052 and nothing else of the namespace; the world, other applications and the job pods reach none of Ascender's pods, the database or the operator; and no monitor scrapes anything of Ascender but the database

  Scenario: Ascender is reached by its own hostname on the public Gateway with no sign-in in front, and its probe asks an endpoint that answers without a session
